# .nodusgate-allow — nodus_gate suppression list
#
# Format: blockhash:<relative-path>:<12-hex>  or  symbol:<name>
#
# The hash covers the code block's normalized source (trailing whitespace and
# surrounding blank lines stripped, line-ending agnostic), so an entry survives
# edits elsewhere in the same file. Edit the block itself and the entry stops
# matching — which is the point: the suppression should be re-reviewed when the
# code it excuses changes.
#
# The legacy `block:<relative-path>:<line>` form is still accepted, but do not
# add new ones. Line numbers rot silently: any edit that inserts a line above a
# block leaves the entry pointing at the wrong place, where it matches nothing
# and suppresses nothing. Four consecutive doc PRs had to re-point entries
# before this file was migrated, and dead entries accumulated unnoticed.
#
# To regenerate after intentional edits:
#     python -m tools.nodus_gate.cli --migrate-allowlist
# It resolves each legacy entry to the block currently at that line, rewrites it
# as a hash, and drops entries that no longer match a runnable block.
#
# Only suppress findings that are intentional (multi-file examples,
# context-dependent snippets, intentional error demos).

